# Calendars, clocks and astronomy, one stanza per program.

shot    cal
cap     A month, one line a week.  -m and -y give the month and year; -h
cap     marks the holidays kept in SYS, and anything added to SYS/birthdays
cap     is included with them.
try     cal -m=9 -y=2026 -h
run     cal -m=9 -y=2026 -h
wait    4

shot    calen
size    90 160
cap     A month as a diary page, ruled for appointments, with the previous
cap     and next months as small calendars in the corners.  It reads the
cap     month, the year and how many months to print from its input.
try     echo 9 2026 1 | calen
os9     echo 9 2026 1 ! calen
run     echo 9 2026 1 | calen
wait    100
snap

shot    calender
size    58 100
cap     A whole year at once, in German, with the German public holidays
cap     listed under it. It asks `Fuer welches Jahr?' -- for which year --
cap     and then asks again for the next.
try     calender
run     calender
wait    2
send    2026\r
wait    6
snap
send    \r
wait    2

shot    digclk
pages
cap     A clock in block digits, redrawn every second with `digclk 1': the
cap     name your system's init module gives it above -- here the host's --
cap     and the date written out below in romanised Japanese, Nihon-go, to
cap     begin with.  Ctrl-C steps to the next language, and on through French,
cap     Spanish, Czech, Roman numerals, Julian, Pig Latin, Unix and English;
cap     Ctrl-E stops it.  It reads TERM, so set that at your shell.
try     digclk 1
run     clear
wait    1
run     digclk 1
wait    5
snap
send    \003
wait    4
snap

shot    gcl
cap     The last frame of a six-second run: the time drawn large across the
cap     terminal.  -s scrolls the digits and -i inverts the video; without -n
cap     it runs until stopped.
try     gcl -n=6
run     gcl -n=6
wait    4
snap
wait    4

shot    ephem
cap     The live panel, from the configuration and star database in SYS:
cap     date, sidereal time and site at the top, and below it each body's
cap     right ascension, declination, azimuth, altitude and more,
cap     recomputed as it runs. RETURN passes the opening page.
try     ephem -c ephem.cfg -d ephem.db
run     builtin cd /dd/SYS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ephem -c ephem.cfg -d ephem.db
wait    8
send    \r
wait    10
snap
quit    \004

shot    ephem881
cap     The same ephemeris built for a 68881 floating-point coprocessor, which
cap     it needs.  In this run its date field reads 9/07/2026 and its calendar
cap     gives September 31 days, while the Julian date and the table agree
cap     with ephem's 24 September.
try     ephem881 -c ephem.cfg -d ephem.db
run     builtin cd /dd/SYS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ephem881 -c ephem.cfg -d ephem.db
wait    8
send    \r
wait    10
snap
quit    \004

shot    lunisolar
cap     The phase of the moon in one line.  Given a year and a time zone
cap     it writes a whole lunisolar calendar as LaTeX instead: the
cap     opening of it is below, one line a day, each one placed by that
cap     day's phase of the moon.
try     lunisolar
run     lunisolar
wait    6
run     lunisolar 2026 EST | head -n 14
wait    6

shot    nasa
cap     Converts NASA two-line orbital elements, the form satellite elements
cap     are published in, into the kepler.dat that `orbit' reads.  Here
cap     nasa.dat, in the current directory, holds one set, for the ISS, pasted
cap     in from a published list: nasa names the satellite as it reads it, and
cap     kepler.dat is what it wrote.
try     nasa
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CAL > /nil 2>/nil; printf "ISS (ZARYA)\r1 25544U 98067A   26246.50000000  .00016717  00000-0  10270-3 0  9005\r2 25544  51.6416 247.4627 0006703 130.5360 325.0288 15.72125391563537\r" > /dd/tmp/CAL/nasa.dat
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/CAL
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat nasa.dat
wait    2
run     nasa
wait    8
run     cat kepler.dat
wait    4

shot    orbit
size    40 80
pages
cap     A satellite tracker: the questions it asks -- a satellite from
cap     kepler.dat, the site, the start date and time, and the step -- then
cap     AO-13 over Pittsburgh through a morning in March 1990, ten minutes a
cap     line: azimuth and elevation, doppler shift, range, height, the point
cap     beneath the satellite and the transponder mode it is in.  It reads its
cap     files by name from the current directory, and DOC/orbit holds them, so
cap     run it there.
try     orbit
run     builtin cd /dd/DOC/orbit
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     orbit
wait    10
send    d\r
wait    4
send    pgh\r
wait    4
send    3 10 90\r
wait    4
send    0\r
wait    4
send    1\r
wait    4
send    10\r
wait    4
snap
send    \r
wait    20
snap

shot    qt
cap     The time the way a person says it: `just gone ten past four',
cap     `nearly a quarter past'.  It says whatever the clock says, and
cap     near the hour or the quarter hour it chimes too.
try     qt
run     qt
wait    5

shot    setimex
super
cap     Checks the system clock against a hardware time source and
cap     corrects it if it drifted.  Run bare, it reports the time it
cap     found -- and that is all it can do under os9exec, where the call
cap     that sets the time does nothing and reports success.  On your own
cap     system, `-s=<date>' sets the clock.
try     setimex
run     setimex
wait    8

shot    today
cap     The date, the time and the moon's phase, spelt out in three sentences.
try     today
run     today
wait    6

shot    greg
cap     A Julian day number to a Gregorian date.  Its count runs one day
cap     ahead of the astronomers' convention: 2440588, which they call
cap     the first of January 1970, comes out as the second.
try     greg 2461281
run     greg 2461281
wait    6
run     greg 2440588
wait    6

shot    almanac
size    24 80
cap     Noon UT on 1 January 1995: right ascension, declination, apparent
cap     diameter and distance for the Sun, Moon and planets.  The Moon's row
cap     is printed to fewer places, the dashes filling out the columns: its
cap     position is computed only to the minute.  Give a longitude and
cap     latitude as well and it adds azimuth and altitude.
try     almanac 0101.1995 12.0000
run     clear
wait    1
run     almanac 0101.1995 12.0000
wait    8

shot    calcdate
size    24 80
cap     Thirty days after 17 May 1988, and the days from 1 January to
cap     31 December 1988.  Dates are mmddyy, with two-digit years.
try     calcdate 051788 -o 30; calcdate 010188 -d 123188
run     clear
wait    1
run     calcdate 051788 -o 30; calcdate 010188 -d 123188
wait    3

shot    ticktalk
size    24 80
cap     A quarter to two in the afternoon in English, French and Afrikaans.
cap     With no time given it tells the time now.
try     ticktalk 13 45; ticktalk -french 13 45; ticktalk -afrikaans 13 45
run     clear
wait    1
run     ticktalk 13 45; ticktalk -french 13 45; ticktalk -afrikaans 13 45
wait    3

shot    weekday
size    24 80
cap     30 October 1989 in weekday's own sentence, and with -v the same facts
cap     without the words between them.
try     weekday 10/30/89; weekday -v 10/30/89
run     clear
wait    1
run     weekday 10/30/89; weekday -v 10/30/89
wait    3
